From ab6ee0e142ffa6ccdc5abbc1bc3dc45a6a005c7e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: adbtrr_work5 <adbtrr_work5@local>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 03:04:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] mm/backtrace: add MM_BACKTRACE allocation call-path recording

Completes the heap backtrace feature on top of the heapinfo capture window
work.  That change added the sched_backtrace() implementation and a two-level
caller record, but the surrounding infrastructure was missing: CONFIG_SCHED_BACKTRACE
had no Kconfig entry anywhere in the tree, so sched_backtrace.c compiled to an
empty translation unit and every recorded frame was NULL.

Ported from the NuttX CONFIG_MM_BACKTRACE feature.

Kconfig
 - os/kernel/Kconfig: new SCHED_BACKTRACE, so the EHABI unwind engine can
   actually be selected.
 - os/mm/Kconfig: new MM_BACKTRACE (depth, default -1 = off),
   MM_BACKTRACE_SEQNO, MM_BACKTRACE_DEFAULT and MM_BACKTRACE_SKIP.
 - os/arch/Kconfig: new ARCH_HAVE_BACKTRACE, selected by an architecture that
   really provides up_backtrace().

Node layout (os/include/tinyara/mm/mm.h)
 - mm_allocnode_s gains seqno and backtrace[CONFIG_MM_BACKTRACE], placed after
   'size' so 'size' keeps the same offset in both node types and the fields
   overlap the flink/blink area of a free chunk.
 - mm_freenode_s mirrors them as reserved padding, preserving the allocator
   invariant SIZEOF_MM_FREENODE >= SIZEOF_MM_ALLOCNODE.
 - Both are rounded up to a MM_MIN_CHUNK multiple, and SIZEOF_MM_ALLOCNODE /
   SIZEOF_MM_FREENODE now derive from sizeof() so they track the real layout
   including padding.  The rounding is applied only when the feature is
   enabled, so a default build keeps its current node sizes byte for byte.

Recording
 - MM_ADD_BACKTRACE() selects the engine: precise EHABI unwind through
   sched_backtrace() for user-space and flat builds, sequence number only for
   the kernel heap of a protected build (the unwinder is not safe in
   early-boot, IDLE or IRQ context), arch up_backtrace() otherwise.
 - Called from mm_malloc(), mm_memalign() and both mm_realloc() paths.
 - mm_backtrace.c provides a weak up_backtrace() so a user-space link without
   an arch unwinder resolves instead of failing.

Reporting
 - mm_heapinfo_backtrace.c walks the heap and prints one line per live
   allocation with pid, task name, address, size, sequence number and the
   recorded call path.
 - Reachable through the mminfo ioctl as HEAPINFO_DETAIL_BACKTRACE, exposed as
   "heapinfo -t" (combine with -p PID for a single task).

Build fixes
 - lib/libc/sched/Make.defs: build sched_backtrace.c only when the feature is
   selected, instead of unconditionally compiling an empty file.
 - amebasmart and imx6 no longer tie the arch up_backtrace() sources to
   CONFIG_SCHED_BACKTRACE.  The two are unrelated, and arm_backtrace_thumb.c
   needs the _stext_flash/_etext_flash linker symbols that not every board
   exports.
 - -funwind-tables is now gated on CONFIG_SCHED_BACKTRACE and excluded from
   flat builds.  A flat build compiles no directory with __KERNEL__, so the
   findstring test alone cannot isolate user space and the flag landed on the
   whole image; unwind tables on RAM-resident kernel text produce .ARM.exidx
   entries whose R_ARM_PREL31 relocation to the far RAM address overflows at
   link time.  This is what breaks the rtl8730e flat_* configurations.
 - mm_memalign.c: the aligned-address search advanced its cursor with pointer
   arithmetic on struct mm_allocnode_s *, scaling every step and bound by the
   node size.  Corrected to byte arithmetic; the bug is latent today and gets
   worse as the node header grows.

Verified by compiling the touched translation units with the feature off, at
depth 0, and at depth 4 with and without SCHED_BACKTRACE, in both flat and
protected (user and kernel pass) configurations.  With MM_BACKTRACE at its
default the node layout and every struct size are unchanged.
